>>
endobj
333 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 171 0 R
/E (Here is a fact that surprised me when I first came across it: most of the worst software vulnerabilities in history did not involve anything clever. No exotic exploit, no zero-day genius. Just a program touching a piece of memory it had no business touching. C and C++ hand that responsibility entirely to the programmer, and history shows programmers keep getting it wrong, not because they are careless but because the mistake is genuinely easy to make and hard to catch by eye.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
334 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 171 0 R
/E (This article walks through why that keeps happening, how Rust manages to catch these mistakes before the program even runs, where it is already being used in real systems, and where it still falls short. The short version: the industry is slowly moving safety out of the programmer's head and into the compiler.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
335 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 171 0 R
/T (Introduction)
/E (Introduction)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
336 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 171 0 R
/E (Heartbleed was not an isolated event. It fits a pattern that shows up again and again in software written in C and C++. So the question worth asking is not "how do we teach programmers to be more careful," which has already been tried for decades, but whether the tools themselves should stop allowing the mistake in the first place.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
337 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 171 0 R
/E (Back in 2014, a two-line oversight in OpenSSL's heartbeat feature turned into one of the most talked-about bugs of the decade. Heartbleed, as it came to be known, let anyone on the internet quietly read chunks of a server's memory, sometimes catching passwords or private keys in the process. Researchers later found a huge share of popular HTTPS sites were affected when the bug came to light [1]. What makes it uncomfortable in hindsight is how ordinary the mistake was. A missing bounds check. That is all it took.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
