/E (Szekeres and colleagues once described decades of defenses against these bugs as an "eternal war in memory" [2], and that phrase has stuck with me. Stack canaries, address space randomization, and similar tricks all raise the cost of exploiting a bug, but none of them stop the bug from existing. They are patches on a wound that keeps reopening, not stitches that close it.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [12]
>>
