350 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 172 0 R
/E (more than a decade, traced back to memory safety issues [3]. Chromium's security team has reported a similar share. Google's Android team tracked the trend over time and found something genuinely encouraging: the memory-safety share of vulnerabilities dropped from 76 percent in 2019 to 24 percent in 2024 [4], mostly because new code was being written in memory-safe languages rather than because old code was rewritten.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
351 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 172 0 R
/E (Governments have taken notice too, which is a little unusual for a programming-language debate. The NSA put out guidance in 2022 telling organizations to move toward memory-safe languages wherever they reasonably could [5]. Two years later, the White House's Office of the National Cyber Director made a similar argument at the policy level [6]. When a national security agency starts commenting on which language you should compile your code in, it is a sign the issue stopped being academic a while ago.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
352 0 obj
<<
