/P 172 0 R
/T (How Does Rust Pull This Off Without a Garbage Collector?)
/E (How Does Rust Pull This Off Without a Garbage Collector?)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
353 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 172 0 R
/E (Languages such as Java, Go, and Python solve memory safety with a garbage collector that runs alongside the program and cleans up unused memory. It works, but it costs something: unpredictable pauses and overhead that kernels, drivers, and embedded firmware )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
354 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 172 0 R
/E (usually cannot afford. Rust took a different path. Instead of checking memory at runtime, it checks the rules while the code is still being compiled.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
355 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 172 0 R
/E (The central idea is ownership. Every value in a Rust program has exactly one owner, and the moment that owner goes out of scope, the memory is freed automatically, no manual cleanup required. When code needs to use a value without taking full ownership of it, it borrows it instead. Here is the rule the compiler enforces, and it is stricter than it sounds: you can have as many read-only borrows as you like, or exactly one mutable borrow, but never both at once. That single constraint quietly rules out use-after-free bugs, double frees, and data races in ordinary safe code, because the compiler simply will not let a program compile if it breaks the rule. Lifetimes push this idea further by making sure a reference can never outlive the data it points to.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
