/E (A world model is an AI system that learns a compact, internal picture of how an environment behaves, so it can predict what happens next, including what happens if the agent itself acts. Systems such as DreamerV3, V-JEPA 2 and Genie 3 now show that agents can learn, plan and even rehearse inside learned models. The article follows the idea from its roots in cognitive science to current systems, separating what each system has demonstrated from what is still a hope. It explains how world models work, how they differ from large language models and conventional machine learning, where they are being tested in robotics, driving and games, and where they still fall short. The question running through it is simple: what changes when AI learns not just from the world, but about how the world behaves?)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
439 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (Keywords: world models, model-based reinforcement learning, latent representations, simulation, embodied AI)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
440 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 175 0 R
/T (World Models: Teaching Artificial Intelligence to Understand, Predict and Simulate the Real World)
/E (World Models: Teaching Artificial Intelligence to Understand, Predict and Simulate the Real World)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
441 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (Abstract)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
442 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (Introduction)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
443 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (Much of modern AI works differently. Image classifiers learned to label photos, and large language models learned to continue text with remarkable fluency. Both are pattern recognisers: given what they have seen, they predict what usually comes next in their data. That is powerful, but it is not the same as understanding why things change, or what would happen if I did something different.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
444 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (How did we get here? Early AI relied heavily on hand-written rules. Statistical machine learning then let programs learn from labelled examples, deep learning scaled that approach to images and speech, and language models extended it to text at enormous scale. Each step made systems better at finding regularities in data. Far fewer systems were built to ask what the world will look like after an action, which is the question world models put first.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
445 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
446 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (Picture a child watching a ball roll toward the edge of a table. Before it drops, she is already reaching out to catch it. Nobody gave her a physics lecture. Her brain simply carries a rough model of how balls, tables and gravity behave, and it runs that model a second or two into the future.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
