453 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (tSo here is the question this article follows: what happens when an AI stops only recognising patterns and starts building an internal model of how its world behaves?)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
454 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (The sections ahead follow the idea in a practical order: what a world model is, how it works and how it is built, where it is being used, and how much of the excitement the evidence supports so far.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
455 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (Understanding World Models)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
456 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (A world model is a learned model of how an environment changes over time, especially in response to an agent's actions. It answers questions of the form: given the situation now, and given that I do this, what is likely to happen next?)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
457 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (LeCun opens his 2022 position paper with a puzzle. A teenager can learn to drive in roughly twenty hours of practice, yet machine-learning systems need vastly more data and trials to approach comparable reliability. His suggested explanation is that humans and animals learn internal models of how the world works [3].)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
458 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (These are tendencies, not walls. Some world models borrow the same transformer building blocks as language models, and how much of a world model a language model builds implicitly is still debated. The distinction that matters is the training goal: a world model is built to predict consequences, so an agent can rehearse a decision in its head before paying for a mistake in reality.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
459 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (Two details make this harder than it sounds. First, the world is only partly observable, so a model has to fill in what its sensors miss, such as what lies behind a parked van. Second, the future is rarely a single outcome. )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
460 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (LeCun's example is a car approaching a fork in the road, where either branch is plausible [3]. A good world model has to represent that uncertainty instead of committing to one guess.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
461 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (Why treat this as a research direction rather than a rebranding? Because it targets three bottlenecks at once: learning from limited interaction, acting safely where mistakes are costly, and planning ahead instead of only reacting. Ha and Schmidhuber built that separation in from the start. The world model absorbs what the agent has seen, so the part that chooses actions can stay very small [2].)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
462 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 176 0 R
/T (How World Models Work)
/E (How World Models Work)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
463 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (A world model runs a loop: sense, compress, predict, imagine, choose, act. Think about crossing a busy road. You glance at the traffic, form a picture of where each vehicle is and how fast it is moving, imagine the next few seconds, and only then step out. A world model gives a machine the same habit, in six steps:)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
464 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1469 0 R
/E (Perception. Raw sensor data arrives, such as camera frames, lidar or joint angles. Much of it is detail the decision does not need.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
465 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1470 0 R
/E (Internal representation. An encoder compresses the observation into a compact latent state, a short vector that keeps what matters \(where the car is\) and drops what does not \(the pattern on a leaf\).)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
466 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1471 0 R
/E (Prediction. A dynamics model takes the current latent state and a proposed action, then predicts the next state. It can also predict quantities such as reward or risk.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
