474 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (The original agent split these jobs into a vision model, a memory model and a small controller [2]. DreamerV3 uses a recurrent state-space world model with separate actor and critic networks [4].)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
475 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (Different systems draw these boxes differently. MuZero's model has three connected parts, called the representation, dynamics and prediction functions, and its hidden state only has to support good estimates of reward, policy and value [5]. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
476 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (LeCun's proposal adds a cost module that scores how "uncomfortable" a state is, a short-term memory, and a configurator that sets up the other modules for the task at hand [3]. In DreamerV3 the recurrent state doubles as memory and as part of the dynamics model, and the inputs are also reconstructed during training so that the latent state keeps the information it needs [4].)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
477 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1477 0 R
/E (Simulation. Chaining predictions lets the model roll out an imagined future several steps ahead without touching the real environment.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
478 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1478 0 R
/E (Planning. The agent compares imagined futures for different action sequences and prefers the one that scores best against its goal. Dreamer-style agents learn behaviour from imagined trajectories [4], while MuZero runs tree search inside its learned model [5].)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
479 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1479 0 R
/E (Decision-making. The agent executes an action, observes what really happened, updates its state and repeats.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
480 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (That last step matters. Because the agent keeps re-observing the world, reality constantly corrects the model, and small prediction errors have less room to snowball.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
481 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (Training comes first, though. In Ha and Schmidhuber's setup, the vision and memory parts learned from observations gathered by random play, without a reward signal, and only afterwards was the small controller trained on top of their compressed features [2].)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
482 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E ( The environment produces an observation, and the encoder compresses it into a latent state that also carries memory of what came before. The dynamics model reads that state and predicts what follows from a candidate action. The planner \(or policy\) proposes actions, receives the predicted outcomes and repeats this exchange inside the model until one action looks best. Only that action is sent back to the world, and the cycle starts again.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
483 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 177 0 R
/T (Technologies Behind World Models)
/E (Technologies Behind World Models)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
484 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (Modern world models combine ideas that matured separately. Five of them do most of the work:)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
