552 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 181 0 R
/E (Background: Large Language Models \(LLMs\) have traditionally been closely associated with cloud computer architectures that depend on large data centers, high-bandwidth computer networks, and massive computing resources. But recent advances in model architecture, quantization, inference engines, and hardware acceleration are disrupting this model. Smaller and smaller LLMs, that can perform increasingly well, are now available on desktops and edge devices, thus eliminating the need for remote servers. )
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
553 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 181 0 R
/E (It compares cloud vs local inference across a range of practical categories, from privacy and latency to cost and capability. Limitations of computing, memory, model capability, power use and security are discussed. It concludes that a hybrid architecture where AI is partitioned between device, edge, and cloud clouds may be the dominant deployment model of the future.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
