/E (text, write code, summarize, extract and analyze data, and converse in natural language. But in essence all of this functionality is still based on cloud technology, where user requests are sent to cloud data centres to be processed by large-scale GPU-based AI models. )
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
559 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 181 0 R
/E (While this approach delivers extensive computational capabilities, it does impose reliance on network connectivity, cloud infrastructure, service availability, and the processing of data by external sources. )
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
560 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 181 0 R
/E (For certain use-cases that require data sovereignty, regulatory compliance, or independent operation, this reliance may be a significant barrier.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
561 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 181 0 R
/E (The Other Way While there are certainly still powerful applications of cloud AI, an alternative approach is now gaining traction; that is, to run the language models locally. While local AI does not necessarily refer to running the largest possible model, it is increasingly heading towards smaller and more capable models within limited computing resources. In modern inference frameworks, it is increasingly common to run quantized models on CPUs and GPU's while platforms like LM Studio make local model execution possible on macOS, Windows and Linux.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
562 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 181 0 R
/E (That signals a significant change in how AI systems are deployed \(the computing can be done where the data is generated rather than as a cloud service\).)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
