571 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (Neural networks normally store model parameters using numerical representations such as 16-bit or 32-bit floating-point values. Quantization reduces the numerical precision used to represent these parameters, thereby reducing memory requirements and often accelerating inference.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
572 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (GPTQ \(Frantar et al., 2022\), which uses second-order information based on approximate Hessian matrices to quantize weights layer-wise. This approach has shown that at the GPT scale \(175bn parameters\), it is probable to compress models down to 3-4 bits with very little impact on accuracy, for example, 4-bit quantized OPT-175B achieved a perplexity increase of just over 0.03 points on Wiki Text-2 [4].)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
573 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (In addition, the Activation-aware Weight Quantization \(AWQ\) method \(Lin et al. 2023\) assumes that, from the activation distribution, about 1% of the model  Therefore, AWQ is expected to preserve higher quality than naive approaches at 4-bit without using backpropagation by protecting important channels with per-channel scaling [12].)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
574 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (For example, the method called SqueezeLLM by Kim et al. \(2023\) combines sensitivity-based non-uniform quantization with sparse storage of outlier weights, which achieves sub-4-bit \(3-bit\) compression and preserves the most sensitive weight values at higher precision [13].)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
575 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 182 0 R
/T ( From Giant Models to Small Language Models)
/E ( From Giant Models to Small Language Models)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
576 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (The traditional way of improving LLM capabilities has typically been according to the paradigm of scale: bigger models, more training data, and more compute. But at some point, such huge models become unfathomably large for local deployment, making them inaccessible and unmanageable to the average user. )
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
577 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (A 70-billion-parameter FP16 model uses about 140 gigabytes of memory; that's far beyond the capabilities of anything currently in the hands of the average user. )
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
578 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (This need has led to research around Small Language Models, Model Patches, and more efficient model architectures. But the goal isn't simply smaller models: SLMs strive to find a trade-off across multiple axes of performance, including model capability, memory, latency, energy, and context length.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
579 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (Newer SLMs have also shown the possibility that quality training data can offset smaller parameter sizes, as exemplified by Microsoft's Phi series, where models trained on curated "textbook-quality" synthetic data perform more strongly than expected in proportion to parameters; Phi-1 with 1.3 B parameters scored 50.6% on Human Eval for code generation, and Phi-3 with 3.8 B parameters was explicitly optimized for phone hardware and performs comparably to models such as Mixtral 87B in some benchmark tasks; Google's Gemma models, 2 B and 7 B, followed on from work by the Gemini project to set benchmarks.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
